"""Objective-specific signals, transport and readiness investigations."""
ROWS=r'''
01.a|In an original single-thread child, block SIGUSR1, generate it twice, inspect pending membership, then synchronously accept it. Contrast a counted application event queue; restore the original mask and consume no unrelated signals.|Pending standard signals form an unlimited counted queue.|Pending membership is not a count; standard-signal coalescing prevents inferring two application events. Generation, eligibility and delivery are distinct states.|Replace the standard signal with a bounded real-time-signal experiment and record queue-capacity/failure semantics.
01.b|Install a sigaction handler that only records a permitted flag, run a bounded blocking operation with and without its applicable restart policy, and retain actual return/progress. Never infer restart behavior from an unrelated syscall.|SA_RESTART restarts every interrupted operation identically.|Restart behavior is operation-specific, and positive partial progress is not undone. The program separately checks the shutdown predicate.|Replace the blocking operation with a deadline wait and preserve the original absolute timeout across interruptions.
01.c|Audit an intentionally flawed handler calling printf, malloc and a mutex. Replace it with a saved-errno, bounded nonblocking self-pipe notification or synchronous signal acceptance. Simulate a full pipe and specify how the durable predicate survives a lost notification byte.|Thread-safe functions are automatically async-signal-safe.|Reentrancy into library state is a distinct constraint; notification failure must not discard the actual shutdown state. A handler allowlist cites exact applicable contracts.|Move signal handling to a dedicated sigwait thread and identify which ordinary-thread services become legal and which ownership obligations remain.
02.a|Enumerate the schedule check-false, signal-handler-set, pause and show the permanent sleep window. Repair with atomic mask-and-wait; compare a self-pipe and Linux signalfd on wake state, portability and descriptor ownership.|Volatile closes the check-then-sleep race.|The repaired operation atomically changes delivery eligibility while waiting; visibility of a flag alone does not make those transitions indivisible.|Add a second reason to wake and show that consuming one notification cannot erase the other predicate.
02.b|Block the chosen termination signal before creating two workers, verify inherited masks, and give one dedicated receiver ownership. Inject a mistakenly unblocked worker in a deterministic model and trace process-directed delivery.|One thread's mask sets the mask for the entire process.|Masks are per-thread while disposition is process-wide; every eligible thread affects delivery. Worker creation inherits the creator's current mask.|Add a thread created by a plugin after initialization and require an explicit masking contract at that boundary.
02.c|Drive shutdown and child-exit notifications through the same bounded loop. Coalesce multiple SIGCHLD notifications deliberately, reap all available original children with nonblocking wait, and recheck the shutdown predicate before sleeping.|One SIGCHLD notification corresponds to exactly one reap.|Notifications indicate work may exist; draining/rechecking state closes coalescing and arrival windows. A child result remains owned until reaped.|Make the final child exit between the last reap and sleep and derive the atomic wait/mask transition that prevents a lost wakeup.
03.a|Carry a fixed request of 8 bytes and a 4 byte result through an owned pipe pair, local socket pair and shared-record model. Tabulate framing, copy ownership, cleanup and synchronization after peer disappearance.|Transport choice can be made from nominal bandwidth alone.|Pipes/streams require framing, message sockets preserve record boundaries, and shared memory needs publication and failure protocols. Each has different backpressure ownership.|Require independent process restart and reassess which state must be named, persisted or reinitialized.
03.b|Define request states allocated, queued, accepted, completed and abandoned with exactly one buffer owner. Inject disconnect before and after acceptance and return an explicit uncertain outcome when processing may have happened.|Disconnect automatically returns ownership and proves no side effect.|Transport closure does not establish application completion or revoke an already borrowed buffer; the protocol needs terminal ownership rules.|Allow cancellation after acceptance and distinguish acknowledgment of the request from confirmation that the original work stopped.
03.c|Compare bounded queues of four requests under a stalled consumer using the three IPC designs. Measure only equivalent successful workloads; separately report memory retained, blocking point and rejection behavior.|Higher throughput implies stronger isolation or better overload behavior.|Queue bounds and ownership determine overload behavior independently of steady-state throughput. Shared memory trades copies for coordination complexity.|Require a strict per-request latency budget and explain why unbounded blocking or retry violates it even at high average throughput.
04.a|Implement local-only connection setup over two address candidates: first deliberately unavailable, second an original listener. Own every socket and free the resolved address list; use a bounded deadline and no public address.|The first getaddrinfo result is necessarily usable.|Candidate iteration and per-attempt cleanup are required. A connection's success is recorded only after the selected operation's completion contract.|Use a nonblocking connect path and inspect SO_ERROR after readiness instead of treating writability as success.
04.b|Inject socket, bind, listen, accept and connect failures through a declared adapter. At each transition compare the live-descriptor ledger to its initial state and ensure no accepted child handle is closed twice.|Failed setup has no resources to release.|Earlier acquisitions survive later failures; ownership transfers at a specific successful step, and close-on-exec is established at creation when supported.|Add cancellation between accept and registration and prove the accepted handle is reclaimed or transferred exactly once.
04.c|Build an address representation table for IPv4 loopback, IPv6 loopback and a private AF_UNIX path. Preserve family-specific length and byte-order rules; test unsupported-family return without guessing layouts.|All socket addresses have one fixed size and byte order.|Address-family tags and lengths determine interpretation; host integers, network fields and local pathnames have different representations.|Replace a pathname local socket with a Linux abstract address at analysis depth and identify its nonportable naming/lifetime assumptions.
05.a|For frames consisting of one length byte followed by at most 8 payload bytes, generate every split of two adjacent frames, including an empty frame and embedded zero. Run one incremental parser and compare to an independent complete-buffer decoder.|One send call produces one receive call.|Stream chunking has no message boundary contract; parser state spans header/body boundaries and must consume each byte once.|Change the header to a two-byte big-endian length and derive partial-header state and overflow checks anew.
05.b|Half-close the original client's write direction after its last complete frame while continuing to receive a response. Inject EOF mid-header and mid-body, then reconnect with an explicit replay policy and fresh parser state.|Receiving EOF invalidates both directions or safely resumes the old parser on reconnect.|Half-close affects one direction; truncated application frames fail separately. Reconnect creates a new transport session and does not resolve uncertain old effects.|Add request identities and recover a lost reply without blindly executing the operation twice.
05.c|Use at most 4 connections,8 queued frames per connection and 8 payload bytes per frame. Stop a peer reading, exercise partial sends and enforce admission/backpressure without allocating beyond the declared bound.|A bounded frame size makes total memory bounded.|Total retained memory includes connection count, queue count, metadata and partially sent frames; each limit needs a rejection or suspension policy.|Add a producer faster than all peers and derive a global budget rather than merely per-connection bounds.
06.a|Send a 12 byte synthetic datagram into an 8 byte receive buffer using recvmsg, retain the truncation indication, and reject it as a complete application frame. Include a legitimate zero-length datagram.|A returned prefix is a complete shorter message, and zero always means stream EOF.|Datagram boundaries and truncation are explicit; discarded suffix bytes cannot be read as continuation. Zero-length datagrams differ from stream closure.|Use an ancillary-data buffer that is too small and handle MSG_CTRUNC separately from payload truncation.
06.b|Implement a bounded sequence acceptance policy over the scripted arrivals 7,9,8,9,6. State the reorder window, duplicate set and loss-report rule before deriving the accepted order and missing evidence.|A sequence gap proves permanent packet loss immediately.|Observed gaps, duplicates and late arrivals depend on the declared window; wrap requires bounded modular comparisons or a new session.|Restart the sender at sequence0 with a new session identity and prevent old delayed packets from entering the new session.
06.c|Let an original receiver discard an accepted datagram after local send succeeds. Add an application acknowledgment with a request ID and classify send success, peer receipt and completed effect separately.|Successful send acknowledges remote application processing.|Local admission to the transport is weaker than delivery or effect; acknowledgments themselves can be lost and retries need idempotence policy.|Lose only the acknowledgment after processing and show why a retry can duplicate a nonidempotent effect.
07.a|Drive read-ready, read2, EAGAIN, read-ready, read1, EOF through an original adapter. Preserve a 3 byte operation cursor and distinguish would-block from terminal error or completion.|Readiness guarantees the requested extent is available.|Readiness permits attempting progress; the next call's result remains authoritative. EAGAIN preserves cursor and owned buffer.|Introduce a second consumer that drains readiness first and require the original loop to tolerate immediate EAGAIN.
07.b|Represent partial receive and transmit separately for one connection. Alternate one-byte progress, cancellation and output backpressure; verify exact bytes and no reuse of a pending output buffer.|An event callback's return ends the lifetime of its operation state.|Operation state lives across callbacks until a terminal transition; input and output can progress independently.|Replace synchronous callbacks with completion events and add generation identity to every retained operation.
07.c|Schedule one always-ready producer and one single-byte request with a per-turn budget of two successful operations. Record service order and demonstrate why unlimited draining or retrying EAGAIN starves the second participant.|An event loop is fair because it is single-threaded.|Fairness requires explicit bounded work and rescheduling; a readiness API does not impose application quotas.|Allow zero-byte application work that still consumes CPU and derive an additional callback/work budget.
08.a|Compare LT with ET for a nonblocking pipe holding 5 bytes and a 2 byte read chunk. Show the ET stall after an incomplete drain, then repair by draining to EAGAIN or retaining an explicit runnable item when a quota expires.|ET gives a fresh notification for every unread byte.|Edges and level predicates differ; quota-limited ET needs user-space readiness state to resume without waiting for a new edge.|Use one-shot registration and derive the rearm point without losing an arrival between drain and rearm.
08.b|Close connection(slot2,generation7), reuse its descriptor number for generation8, then deliver a cached event for 7. Require rejection before dereferencing connection storage; inspect unregister and outstanding-event ownership.|A descriptor integer permanently names a connection.|Reused numbers need independent identity and lifetime validation; removing registration does not erase already copied events.|Exhaust the generation space in a reduced-width model and implement permanent retirement or another explicit anti-wrap policy.
08.c|Compose a per-connection 2 operation quota with a global 8 operation turn budget for three ready connections. Prove every continuously ready connection receives service under the declared round-robin scheduler, and bound callback work separately.|A per-connection quota alone bounds a turn with unlimited connections.|Both population and work limits are needed; the guarantee depends on the ready-list discipline and callback bound.|Permit callbacks to enqueue new ready items and define whether they enter this turn or the next without unbounded recursion.
09.a|Integrate a nonblocking CLOCK_MONOTONIC timerfd and an eventfd counter in the original loop. Read exact counter widths, preserve accumulated counts, and handle EAGAIN without treating it as cancellation.|A notification descriptor carries one Boolean event per read.|Counters accumulate multiple events; decoding native counters is distinct from protocol byte order. Descriptor ownership and full-width reads are explicit.|Replace periodic wakeups with a single earliest-deadline timer and derive rearm behavior when an earlier deadline is inserted.
09.b|Record scheduled expiry, kernel-reported expiration count and actual dispatch time while deliberately stalling only the teaching loop. Compare a late dispatch with the abstract deadlines fixture without assigning physical accuracy to it.|The callback timestamp equals the expiration time.|Expiry and handling are separated by scheduling/work; accumulated periodic expirations do not imply that many handlers ran on time.|Change from catch-up to skip-missed-periods policy and derive effect count and next deadline.
09.c|Cancel and reschedule a timer object, then deliver a cached notification from its old generation. Make repeated cancellation idempotent and ensure an old event cannot fire the new callback.|Disarming a source retracts every notification already copied into user space.|Registration state and queued callback identity are separate; generation checks and retirement protect the new object.|Let cancellation race with an already-running callback and specify whether cancellation waits, fails or returns an explicit still-running status.
10.a|Configure only a newly owned PTY pair for 8N1-style service parameters and explicit raw/noncanonical read policy, read back supported settings, and restore on exit. Keep the actual baud claim pending on a physical UART.|A successful termios call proves electrical baud/framing on a PTY.|PTYs test line discipline and byte transport, not clocks, voltage levels or real serial framing. Readback checks accepted settings.|Use VMIN>0/VTIME>0 and predict a no-first-byte wait separately from the interbyte timeout.
10.b|Reuse the same profile-C parser object with byte-array, PTY and local-socket adapters. Replay the exact same segmentation/error corpus while keeping OS allocation and I/O outside the parser closure.|A transport adapter may silently reset or enlarge parser state to make tests pass.|The portable parser contract and resource bound remain invariant; adapters translate only transport progress/failure semantics.|Deliver a reconnect mid-frame and specify the parser reset policy at the service boundary.
10.c|Design a virtual SocketCAN test using only an already provisioned isolated vcan interface or a frame adapter if unavailable. Define standard/extended identifiers, length, loopback and error-frame policy; never open an operational vehicle or deployed bus.|A virtual CAN frame test establishes bus arbitration or physical reliability.|Virtual/adapted frames test software interpretation only; bit timing, transceiver and actual arbitration remain physical obligations.|Move the contract to a dedicated two-node teaching bench and enumerate the new electrical/termination/bit-timing evidence before execution.
11.a|For the bounded 4 connection service, reject the fifth admission and the ninth queued frame per connection with explicit ownership return. Compare drop-newest, disconnect and pause-reading policies on the same overloaded trace.|Allocation failure is the only necessary load-shedding policy.|Admission and queue bounds should prevent uncontrolled retention before exhaustion; every rejected object remains owned somewhere.|Add variable-sized frames and derive a byte budget rather than relying on object count alone.
11.b|Implement stop-admission, drain-until-deadline, cancel-remaining and release states. Give each queued item a unique ID and verify exactly one completed or canceled terminal record under repeated stop requests.|Closing the listener automatically settles accepted work.|Accepted connections, pending output and callbacks survive listener closure; terminal accounting and quiescence control destruction.|Permit a callback to request shutdown reentrantly and defer destruction until all current borrowers return.
11.c|Force an empty blocked consumer, a full blocked producer, disappearing peer and repeated shutdown notifications. Use explicit enrollment barriers in tests instead of sleeping to guess when a waiter is blocked.|A delay in the test proves the desired interleaving occurred.|Deterministic enrollment makes the schedule evidence meaningful; stress delay alone is probabilistic. Shutdown must wake both directions under the chosen policy.|Introduce a timeout concurrent with shutdown and specify the single winning result plus cleanup responsibility.
12.a|Required expansion: use the retained T62 submission/completion/cancellation contracts to implement a bounded io_uring read adapter only after pinning installed operation/version support. Keep buffers live through the target completion, distinguish cancel CQE from target CQE, and report unsupported execution honestly.|Submitting cancellation immediately releases the target buffer.|Submission, cancellation acknowledgment and operation completion have different identities/lifetimes. A model pass is not a real io_uring operation observation.|Switch to a registered-buffer or multishot operation only after inspecting its distinct lifetime and completion-flag contract.
12.b|Compare ordinary copying with a selected sendfile/splice or registered-buffer path over the same original file/socket workload. Count retained buffers, pin duration, bookkeeping and retries; label any kernel copy claim by evidence source.|Zero-copy means zero memory traffic and no lifetime cost.|Copy avoidance changes where ownership and resource retention live; payload equality alone does not measure hidden copies or pinning costs.|Make the receiver stall and derive the maximum retained memory and cancellation latency for each path.
12.c|Benchmark readiness and completion backends on identical bounded request/result contracts, warm/cold data states and declared queue depths. Include latency distribution, CPU use, memory and failure cleanup; publish a sealed decision before seeing the alternate result.|The newest backend must be faster for every workload.|Measurements are workload/environment-specific and must include equivalent correctness/failure obligations; unsupported cells remain gaps.|Change from large sequential transfers to small scattered reads and predict which cost dominates before repeating the experiment.
'''
def rows():
 return {'T24.'+a:dict(task=b,misconception=c,expected=d,variant=e) for a,b,c,d,e in (line.split('|') for line in ROWS.strip().splitlines())}
